# po-s148 — decision-consumption cycle 2026-07-21T18:11Z
# Five PO rulings, no fresh batch. ONE new row only (item 2, explicitly recommended).
#   1. FIX-ORCHSTATE-UPDATED-AT-WRITE-PATH  — acceptance waiver: amend criterion (1)
#   2. MINT FIX-BCTC-NEWSCHAIN-FALLBACK-ZEROS-WRITE-TARGET (HIGH, backlog)
#   3. DESIGN-COWORK-FANOUT-...-ORDERING    — architect deviation ACCEPTED, release to pm
#   4. R3 market-watcher slot routing        — FOLD into item-3 row (already T6/T7), sequence T6 first
#   5. SPIKE-COWORK-DRAIN-BODY-...          — CLOSE (question answered), fold remediation
# Usage: jq -f scripts/po-s148-decision-consumption-20260721.jq --arg ts "<iso>" \
#          docs/data/orch/orch-state.json | bash scripts/orch-apply.sh

def upd(lane; tid; patch):
  .task_board[lane] = [ .task_board[lane][] | if (.id == tid) then . + patch else . end ];

# ─── 1. ACCEPTANCE WAIVER — updated_by dropped from the chokepoint ────────────
upd("review"; "FIX-ORCHSTATE-UPDATED-AT-WRITE-PATH"; {
  acceptance: "(1) [AMENDED 2026-07-21T18:11Z by po_ruling_updated_by_20260721T1811 — was 'stamps updated_at + updated_by'; updated_by is deliberately OUT OF SCOPE at this chokepoint, see that key for the full reasoning] scripts/orch-apply.sh stamps updated_at on every task row whose content changed between live and candidate, and on NO row that did not change; (2) a write through the wrapper by ANY caller (including a jq transform that never mentions updated_at) produces a stamped row; (3) no historical row is backfilled by this change; (4) the existing validation / conservation / CAS / atomic-rename behaviour is unchanged — reuse, do not reimplement, per the script's own HARD CONSTRAINTS block.",

  po_ruling_updated_by_20260721T1811: "DISPOSITION [c], EXECUTED VIA [a]'s MECHANISM. Ruling: updated_by is DROPPED as a chokepoint responsibility because it is unattainable there AND undesirable there; the acceptance text is amended in the same write so the row's own record no longer asserts a field nothing writes. NOT [b] — see below. Three grounds, each verified at source this tick, not accepted from the report. GROUND 1 — NO CONSUMER EXISTS. Per-task-row updated_by is write-only across the entire system. projectTask() (interface/mcp/routes/orchestrationHandler.ts:162-194) projects id/title/owner/status/zone/type/size/priority/status_note/created_at/closed_at/depends/note/files/commit — and NOT updated_by or updated_at. The dashboard's visible 'Updated By' cell renders head.updated_by via projectHead() (same file, line 157) — a DIFFERENT, head-level field that this row never touched and that is unaffected either way. Per-row updated_by is not even declared in TaskSchema (orchStateSchema.ts:226 and :246 are HeadSchema and MetaSchema respectively); it validates purely through TaskSchema's .passthrough(). And no flow/jq predicate reads it: every one of the 30+ hits under scripts/ is a WRITER (updated_by: \"router\" / \"po-s69\" / \"dev-mcp-server\" ...), zero are select/sort/test predicates. So criterion (1)'s updated_by half would have shipped machinery for a field with no reader in code, no reader in the dashboard, and no reader in any triage predicate. Contrast updated_at, which has real (flow-level) consumers and whose absence caused every harm in this row's why_p0 — the harms are ALL timestamp harms; updated_by carries zero weight in the stated problem and was acceptance-text pairing, not a requirement traced to it. GROUND 2 — STAMPING IT WOULD DESTROY REAL INFORMATION, not merely add noise. 44 rows currently carry caller-supplied attribution that is genuinely high-quality and semantically rich: 'po/triage-20260721T1707', 'pm (decompose SYSREMAKE-P2 Leg-1, session e417ef1f)', 'architect/design-complete-20260721T1804Z', 'po (DECISION-1 flow-alpha full GO — user selection e417ef1f)'. A chokepoint stamp has only two possible behaviours and both are net regressions: overwrite those 44 with a generic token (destroys the best attribution data on the board), or fill only when absent (still fabricates for the other 431). GROUND 3 — THE ASYMMETRY WITH updated_at IS PRINCIPLED, not arbitrary. updated_at is DERIVABLE at the chokepoint from ground truth the chokepoint actually holds: the clock, plus a content diff against the live file. updated_by is NOT derivable there — caller identity is genuinely absent from an anonymous `jq | bash scripts/orch-apply.sh` pipe. Stamping a value you cannot derive is precisely the falsification class this row's own do_not_over_correct block bans for timestamps ('a fabricated value is WORSE than a null one'); the developer applied that ban consistently to the sibling field rather than quietly half-satisfying the criterion, and disclosed it. That is the correct engineering instinct and it is upheld. WHY NOT [b]: threading a caller-identity token (env var / argv) through the wrapper is technically attainable, but it adds a MANDATORY parameter to 30+ existing call sites where the failure mode of a single forgotten token is a hard abort on the ONE mandatory write path for the entire board — a large blast radius and a new class of board-wide wedge, incurred to populate a field with no reader. Rejected on cost/benefit, not on feasibility. CONVENTION PRESERVED, NOT ABOLISHED: callers that CAN attribute honestly SHOULD keep setting updated_by in their own transform, exactly as those 44 do. It stays a voluntary caller-side convention carrying real provenance; what it is not, and will not become, is a chokepoint guarantee. Do not re-propose chokepoint updated_by without first naming a consumer that reads it.",

  po_ruling_lane_agnostic_diff_20260721T1811: "RULED: ACCEPTED — and on reflection it is not merely tolerable, it is the CORRECT semantics, so it needs no follow-up. Router disclosed the diff predicate as lane-agnostic: a row that changes lane with byte-identical field content gets no stamp; the residual uncovered case is a status legal in two lanes (e.g. BLOCKED in both backlog and review) relocated with nothing else touched. Two reasons to accept. (i) The residue is already tiny for the reason developer gave — checkLaneCoherence() (orchStateSchema.ts:414-422, LANE_ALLOWED_STATUSES) constrains every lane to a narrow status set, so a real lane move almost always changes status and the plain content diff catches it. (ii) More importantly, updated_at exists to serve staleness predicates asking 'when did this row's SUBSTANCE last change'. A pure relocation with byte-identical content is not a substance change, and stamping it would make an untouched row look freshly worked — reintroducing, in miniature, exactly the false-negative this row was minted to kill. A lane move that matters always carries a content change. Closing this as a deliberate, correct design choice.",

  po_signoff_note_20260721T1811: "PO acceptance ruling complete — criteria (2)(3)(4) were already MET per router's independent verification (both commits clean, Stage 1.5 correctly ordered after Stage 0/1 and before Stage 2, 42/42 test suite re-run by router, four independent live callers since). Criterion (1) is now MET AS AMENDED. No blocker remains from PO. next_agent/qa and lane unchanged — this ruling clears the waiver question only; the QA pass itself is still QA's to run and close."
})

# ─── 3 + 4. FAN-OUT DESIGN — deviation accepted, R3 folded, T6 sequenced first ─
| upd("ready"; "DESIGN-COWORK-FANOUT-PRODUCER-CONSUMER-ORDERING"; {
  po_ruling_deviation_accepted_20260721T1811: "DEVIATION FROM (a)/(b)/(c) — ACCEPTED. Architect was right to not pick one of my three literals, and the row's own options_for_architect text explicitly said PO had NOT pre-selected one. I verified BOTH brownfield facts the deviation rests on at source rather than accepting them: (F4) docs/agents/cowork-team/flow/last-fired.md:10 states 'AC-P1-7-1: last_fired written after successful spawn' — confirmed, last_fired is stamped at SPAWN, not completion, so any consumer re-deriving co-dispatch from it after the fact would read a slot as fired before its output exists and be silently defeated. That single fact kills the cheap version of (b) and justifies the tick-snapshot won_slots capture instead. (F7) docs/agents/market-watcher/flow/main.md:10-14 routes on wall-clock UTC windows only, and Step 2 ('Read current UTC time. Match the window above') never consumes the slot= the dispatcher already sends — confirmed, the parameter is discarded. Both facts hold. The chosen bounded-async schedule_task recheck is a better option than any of my three: it keeps BGFAN-1's parallel fire-and-forget contract untouched (the real cost of (a), compounded by the fire-election 600s TTL lapse risk during a multi-minute in-session wait), pays zero cost on quiet ticks (the real cost of (b)-as-worded), and does not leave a market-facing miss undefended (the disqualifier for (c)). Brief section 3 states the tradeoffs plainly as the row's acceptance required — including the two I most wanted named: detection latency goes to a bounded ~20min and NOT to zero, and a tick now has two possible verdicts so telemetry readers must check the recheck cycle too. Acceptance MET. Released to pm for the 8-row T1-T8 decomposition.",

  po_ruling_r3_folds_here_20260721T1811: "ITEM 4 RULED — FOLDS INTO THIS ROW, NO SEPARATE ROW MINTED. The market-watcher slot-routing defect (a late-firing market-watcher-eod falls through to the generic offhours cycle and loses eod.md's ledger + signal-file deliverable that Chef's 08:37Z dish consumes) is already decomposed HERE as T6 (+ optional doc-only T7) against brief section 5, and section 5's fix is the correct root-cause one: route by the slot= already sent, wall-clock table as no-slot fallback only. Minting a second row would split one root cause (a receiving flow discarding its dispatched identity and re-deriving from ambient clock) across two owners — the same defect shape as section 4.3, same dispatcher, same tick. I also endorse architect's refusal to flip match-slots.md Step 4b from WARN to BLOCK: the dispatcher cannot distinguish a legitimate two-dish tick from this incident without leaking market-domain knowledge into a generic scheduler, and blocking would break the documented intentional case. SEQUENCING INSTRUCTION FOR PM — T6 GOES FIRST, ahead of T1-T5. Three reasons: (a) T6 is the only strand that is market-facing DATA LOSS rather than detection latency — a lost eod.md deliverable is a missing artifact a downstream Chef dish depends on, strictly worse than an alert arriving ~20min late; (b) brief section 9 already records T6/T7 as having NO dependency on T1-T5 and being separately shippable, so there is no cost to promoting it; (c) T3 is the one strand that touches apps/mcp-server/ and must be sequenced behind the live qa full-suite run, so T1-T5 cannot complete promptly anyway — T6 is doc-only and unblocked right now. Parallelise T6 against T1/T2/T5 if two developers are available.",

  po_scope_hold_20260721T1811: "SCOPE HOLD REAFFIRMED at release. This row still does NOT own the get_alerts(type=price) detector question — important_scoping_do_not_absorb stands unchanged, prior art FIX-ALERT-SCAN-REJECT-STUB-BAR-P0 and FIX-ALERT-OPEN-ZERO-PRICE-RACE remain deliberately un-refiled, and BOTH causes may be live. Landing T1-T8 must NOT be read as closing the detector question by proxy. Architect correctly confirmed the digest-daily flow_path/trigger_prompt signal is orthogonal (different parallel_group, different field) — it does not fold in here either.",

  po_released_to_pm_at: $ts,
  updated_at: $ts,
  updated_by: "po/decision-consumption-20260721T1811"
})

